## roborev: Combined Review (`7009e87`)

**Summary Verdict:** The security posture is robust, but there is one high-severity frontend issue that will cause a runtime error when using the new agent filter.

### High

**Missing `setAgentFilter` method in SessionsStore**
* **Location:**
 `frontend/src/lib/components/layout/AppHeader.svelte` (line 14) and `frontend/src/lib/stores/sessions.svelte.ts`
* **Description:** `AppHeader.svelte` calls `sessions.setAgentFilter(select.value)`, but the 
`setAgentFilter` method is missing/never defined in `SessionsStore` (`sessions.svelte.ts`). This will result in a `TypeError: sessions.setAgentFilter is not a function` when attempting to use the new agent filter UI.
* **Suggested Fix:** Add the missing method to `SessionsStore` in 
`sessions.svelte.ts`. For example:
  ```typescript
  setAgentFilter(agent: string) { 
      this.filters = { ...defaultFilters(), agent, project: this.filters.project }; 
      this.activeSessionId = null; 
      this.resetPagination();

      this.load(); 
  }
  ```

---
*Synthesized from 4 reviews (agents: codex, gemini | types: default, security)*

This is already addressed — the modifier guard exists at **line 65** of `keyboard.ts` (added in commit `daa9e71`):

```typescript
// All remaining shortcuts are plain single-key — skip if any modifier is held.
// (Shift is allowed because "?" requires Shift on most layouts.)
if (e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.altKey) return;
```

This runs **before** the `keyActions` dispatch at line 91, so `Ctrl+C` / `Cmd+C` will never reach the `c` binding. There are also 8 regression tests covering this behavior (`keyboard.test.ts`).

Marking as false positive — no changes needed.

Makes sense to me. The feature is non-invasive, but I don't see a problem with having it for people who want it. 

I really appreciate your contributions, I see that you developed https://github.com/userFRM/llm-deep-trace. I see there is also https://github.com/jazzyalex/agent-sessions but this is macOS only. Given the relatively low cost of developing and iterating on features, it seems healthy and productive for projects to learn from each other and cherry-pick features that have been proven to be useful. 

@mariusvniekerk and I were also discussing building a web UI for roborev within agentsview (for people who use it — since we can just talk to the roborev daemon over HTTP), so we can get session histories and code reviews in a single desktop application, which seems better than having to build and ship multiple Tauri or Electron apps, so that's another thing to noodle on if it's interesting to you.

@clkao is exploring this idea in https://github.com/clkao/agentstrove

I implemented review syncing for roborev with postgres, so I think between these systems we can probably ideate something that can work for agentsview. I'm working on a Tauri desktop app version of the system, so it will need to work within that context too

https://www.roborev.io/guides/postgres-sync/

